# The inherited token layer is a byte-identical copy pinned by SHA-256
# in scripts/check-tokens.mjs (D-12). Git's core.autocrlf would
# silently rewrite its line endings to CRLF on a future checkout on
# this machine, breaking the pinned hash everywhere except the
# already-checked-out copy. Marking it -text disables all newline
# conversion so the bytes survive checkout unchanged.
app/styles/tokens.inherited.css -text

# The four fixture files below are the opposite case: they are
# actively edited and re-pinned through the rest of the project
# (D-14), not pinned once and left alone. `text eol=lf` normalises
# them to LF on checkout and check-in instead of disabling
# normalisation outright. This entry is defence in depth only — it
# acts on checkout and check-in and does not retroactively
# renormalise content already committed with CRLF. The load-bearing
# mechanism is the CRLF-to-LF normalisation inside
# scripts/check-fixture-hash.mjs itself, which recomputes the pinned
# digest over normalised content and so cannot be defeated by an
# editor's line-ending setting.
lib/data/plant.ts text eol=lf
lib/data/artisans.ts text eol=lf
lib/data/orders.ts text eol=lf
lib/data/observations.ts text eol=lf

# scripts/curl-suite.sh (03-14) is a shebang script a reviewer runs
# directly with `bash scripts/curl-suite.sh` from an ordinary Linux or
# macOS shell. A CRLF line ending anywhere inside it — which
# core.autocrlf=true would introduce silently on this machine's next
# checkout, exactly as for the fixture files above — breaks a bash
# script outright (a `\r` surviving inside a heredoc, a quoted string
# or, worst case, the shebang line itself). `text eol=lf` keeps it
# LF-terminated on checkout and check-in; scoped to this one path only,
# per this plan's own instruction.
scripts/curl-suite.sh text eol=lf
